# C++ Backend Rules

Backend-focused rules for cpp projects.

## Style & resource ownership
- Prefer modern C++17/20/23 features over C-style constructs.
- Use `clang-format` and keep formatter settings in the repo.
- RAII everywhere: no manual `new`/`delete`; use `std::make_unique` / `std::make_shared`.
- Use `std::unique_ptr` for exclusive ownership and `std::shared_ptr` only for real shared ownership.
- Prefer Rule of Zero; if custom lifetime methods are required, follow Rule of Five.
- Pass small/trivial types by value, large values by `const&`, and return by value.

## Backend patterns
- Encapsulate file, socket, DB, and other OS handles in RAII wrappers.
- Keep storage behind repository interfaces instead of exposing driver/query-builder details.
- Use `std::optional` for missing values and `std::expected`/result types for expected failures.
- Use exceptions for exceptional states only; map service failures to safe API errors at the boundary.

## Security
- Use `std::string`, `std::array`, and `std::vector`; avoid raw arrays and unsafe C string functions.
- Never use `strcpy`, `strcat`, or `sprintf`; prefer `fmt::format` or safe C++ APIs.
- Always initialize variables; avoid undefined behavior, dangling pointers, and unjustified casts.
- Run sanitizers in CI for service code: AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer.
- Run `clang-tidy` and `cppcheck` on backend/native modules.
- Prefer verified framework/provider auth components over custom crypto, session,
  password, or JWT implementations.

## Testing
- Use GoogleTest/gMock with CMake/CTest.
- Run tests with sanitizers enabled in CI.
- Track coverage with `lcov`/equivalent where C++ backend logic is business-critical.
